<ArticleLayout
  title="ESG Due Diligence for Investors Entering ASM Value Chains"
  description="What a meaningful ESG assessment covers when the asset in question touches artisanal and small-scale mining."
>
  <p>
    Standard ESG due diligence frameworks are usually built with
    large-scale, formally regulated operations in mind: audited
    financials, established environmental permits, unionised or
    formally contracted labour. Applying that template unchanged to an
    asset touching artisanal and small-scale mining tends to miss the
    risks that actually matter there, and can also undervalue
    operations that are genuinely well run by ASM standards but do not
    look like an LSM operation on paper.
  </p>

  <h2>What to look at instead</h2>
  <p>
    Governance, in an ASM context, means understanding who actually
    controls a site and how disputes over land and production get
    resolved. Environmental assessment needs to account for the
    cumulative impact of many small operations across a landscape,
    beyond any single large permitted footprint. Labour review
    should look at safety practice and fair payment on the ground, since
    formal employment contracts are often absent even where the
    underlying work is legitimate and well organised.
  </p>
  <p>
    Traceability and chain-of-custody evidence deserve particular
    attention where an asset sources from or coexists with ASM activity.
    An investor needs a clear answer to where material actually
    originates, and how confidently that can be verified, before pricing
    the risk of a supply chain challenge later.
  </p>

  <h2>Why direct operating experience changes the assessment</h2>
  <p>
    Reviewing an ASM-adjacent asset from documentation alone tends to
    produce two failure modes: overestimating risk in operations that
    are actually well managed, because they lack formal paperwork, and
    underestimating risk in operations that have good paperwork but poor
    practice on the ground. Site-level assessment by people who
    understand how an ASM operation actually functions day to day
    catches both.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the assessment Orivia brings: due diligence informed by
    running an ASM site ourselves, applied to the specific asset an
    investor is evaluating, in the frontier-market conditions that asset
    actually operates in.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
